WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.202
"The National Library
of Medicine presents...

00:00:02.202 --> 00:00:02.769
[Intro Music]

00:00:02.769 --> 00:00:06.673
"What in the world is arsenic?
Arsenic in our food."

00:00:06.673 --> 00:00:08.174
Caleb: This rice is the best!

00:00:08.174 --> 00:00:10.844
Whatever my mom put
in it is so good!

00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:14.013
Gloria: Lucky you,
my lunch is so boring.

00:00:14.013 --> 00:00:16.816
My apple juice is the best part.

00:00:16.816 --> 00:00:19.686
Grace: I dunno you guys;
think about what you're eating.

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:22.489
Caleb, I read online
that arsenic is in rice.

00:00:22.489 --> 00:00:25.091
And Gloria, I heard
it's in apple juice too.

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:27.394
You guys could get
arsenic poisoning!!

00:00:27.394 --> 00:00:30.330
Caleb: I don't know, Grace,
I don't think it's bad.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.998
Tastes pretty delicious to me!

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:34.334
Gloria: I don't know either,
but let's find out!

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:36.035
Caleb: What's arsenic anyway?

00:00:36.035 --> 00:00:39.472
Grace: I remember Mr. Best
naming it on the periodic table.

00:00:39.472 --> 00:00:41.674
That's its chemical symbol.

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:45.745
Gloria: Yeah, he said it was
an odorless, tasteless element.

00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:48.014
Caleb: [alarmed] So how could we
tell if it's in our food??

00:00:48.014 --> 00:00:49.482
Gloria: Chill out, Caleb.

00:00:49.482 --> 00:00:51.851
Let's first figure
out where arsenic is.

00:00:51.851 --> 00:00:54.254
Caleb: It's an element on
the periodic table, right?

00:00:54.254 --> 00:00:57.524
It's probably found in
the earth, water, or air-

00:00:57.524 --> 00:00:59.225
like a bunch of other ones.

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:00.660
Grace: Just found a
great site from the

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.262
National Library of Medicine,

00:01:02.262 --> 00:01:06.733
called the Environmental Health
Student Portal, let's see!

00:01:06.733 --> 00:01:09.602
Grace: It says arsenic is
found in the Earth's crust,

00:01:09.602 --> 00:01:10.870
air, and water.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.640
Caleb: Humans also add arsenic
to the environment through

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:15.375
farming and mining.

00:01:15.375 --> 00:01:16.643
Caleb: People use arsenic in

00:01:16.643 --> 00:01:19.179
wood preservatives
and pesticides.

00:01:19.179 --> 00:01:22.015
Gloria: Arsenic exists
in different forms.

00:01:22.015 --> 00:01:25.652
Pure arsenic is rare; it usually
combines with other elements to

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:28.021
form compounds, or combinations.

00:01:28.021 --> 00:01:30.824
Grace: Yeah, organic
and inorganic compounds.

00:01:30.824 --> 00:01:32.325
They're kinda like
relationships;

00:01:32.325 --> 00:01:34.961
some are more toxic than others.

00:01:34.961 --> 00:01:36.930
Let me explain
with the chalkboard.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.532
Grace: When arsenic combines
with carbon and hydrogen,

00:01:39.532 --> 00:01:42.068
it makes organic
arsenic compounds,

00:01:42.068 --> 00:01:44.737
which are less toxic
than inorganic ones.

00:01:44.737 --> 00:01:46.906
Gloria: When arsenic combines
with another element like

00:01:46.906 --> 00:01:49.776
oxygen or chlorine, it
makes inorganic compounds,

00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:51.945
which are harmful
to human health.

00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:53.947
Caleb: What, I can't
believe I'd never learned

00:01:53.947 --> 00:01:56.049
the science-y words
for this stuff.

00:01:56.049 --> 00:01:59.686
Grace: Caleb, Mr. Best talked
about that stuff two hours ago.

00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:01.221
Caleb: Sorry, I forgot!

00:02:01.221 --> 00:02:02.956
Grace: [incredulous]
From two hours ago?

00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:04.524
Caleb: What, I have
a short memory!

00:02:04.524 --> 00:02:07.594
Gloria: You guys, this
doesn't tell me if I should be

00:02:07.594 --> 00:02:09.963
worried about what's
in my apple juice!

00:02:09.963 --> 00:02:12.565
Is arsenic poisonous or not??

00:02:12.565 --> 00:02:15.001
Caleb: My dad loves
to read mystery books;

00:02:15.001 --> 00:02:17.637
some of their plots
have arsenic poisoning.

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:20.673
Grace: That's not pulled out
of thin air; it can be toxic.

00:02:20.673 --> 00:02:22.876
Bad things have
happened in real life!

00:02:22.876 --> 00:02:25.778
There was a 19th century
Arctic explorer named

00:02:25.778 --> 00:02:27.881
Charles Francis Hall.

00:02:27.881 --> 00:02:29.716
Grace: He was
perfectly healthy until

00:02:29.716 --> 00:02:33.052
he drank a cup of coffee
one day...and died!

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:35.989
People thought he died from a
disease, so no one suspected

00:02:35.989 --> 00:02:39.492
foul play, until almost
100 years later, when

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:41.160
a biographer looked into it.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.297
The guy discovered that Hall
died from a lot of arsenic in

00:02:44.297 --> 00:02:47.333
his body...but the arsenic
was only present in the last

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:48.801
two weeks of his life!

00:02:48.801 --> 00:02:52.872
People say his
crew murdered him.

00:02:52.872 --> 00:02:54.507
Caleb: Wow, that's terrible!

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:55.775
Grace: I know, right!

00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:58.411
Arsenic can be fatal,
but can also cause other

00:02:58.411 --> 00:03:01.414
health problems, like
cancer, skin color changes,

00:03:01.414 --> 00:03:03.016
and small warts.

00:03:03.016 --> 00:03:04.484
Gloria: [heavily sarcastic]
That's not alarming.

00:03:04.484 --> 00:03:05.718
Caleb: [equally
sarcastic] Not at all.

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:08.688
I see how that guy's crew
put arsenic in his coffee,

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:13.359
but how's arsenic in my rice
or Gloria's apple juice?

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:16.329
Grace: Arsenic is in
soil and water, right?

00:03:16.329 --> 00:03:19.399
Some of it can make its way
to drinking water and food,

00:03:19.399 --> 00:03:22.669
since rice, apples, and
other fruits, vegetables, and

00:03:22.669 --> 00:03:24.737
grains are grown in the soil.

00:03:24.737 --> 00:03:27.640
There's a teeny bit of
arsenic in a bunch of food.

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:31.277
Some plants though, like rice,
take up arsenic from the soil

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:33.746
more readily than others.

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:36.115
Gloria: [worried] So
we're in trouble, huh?

00:03:36.115 --> 00:03:37.216
Caleb: Not exactly.

00:03:37.216 --> 00:03:38.851
The dose makes the poison.

00:03:38.851 --> 00:03:42.455
For example, aspirin is helpful
for minor pain relief, and is

00:03:42.455 --> 00:03:46.492
pretty safe in small amounts,
but toxic in large ones.

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:49.696
Not just that, we're barely
exposed to arsenic.

00:03:49.696 --> 00:03:53.166
A teeny bit is in some food, but
it's not like we're mining and

00:03:53.166 --> 00:03:54.667
around high levels of it.

00:03:54.667 --> 00:03:57.503
Grace: How do you know
how much arsenic is okay?

00:03:57.503 --> 00:04:00.540
Gloria: Have you heard of the
Environmental Protection Agency,

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:02.175
or EPA?

00:04:02.175 --> 00:04:03.543
My uncle works there!

00:04:03.543 --> 00:04:06.546
It regulates how much
chemicals are allowed in stuff.

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:08.748
Grace: Let's see what
it says about arsenic.

00:04:08.748 --> 00:04:09.482
Caleb: Got it!

00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:12.085
The EPA allows 10
parts per billion,

00:04:12.085 --> 00:04:16.589
or ppb, of arsenic
in drinking water.

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:21.094
Caleb: 1 ppb is a sugar cube in
an Olympic-size swimming pool.

00:04:21.094 --> 00:04:24.697
If more arsenic than 10
ppb is found in water,

00:04:24.697 --> 00:04:28.901
special water treatment methods
are used to take it out.

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:31.104
Gloria: What about
for apple juice?

00:04:31.104 --> 00:04:32.772
Caleb: Hold your horses,
I'm looking it up.

00:04:32.772 --> 00:04:34.140
[mutters under his breath]
Spotty Wi-Fi.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:34.807
Caleb: Ah ha!

00:04:34.807 --> 00:04:37.410
Another federal agency
called the Food and Drug

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:40.880
Administration, or FDA,
has been checking out

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.815
arsenic levels in food.

00:04:42.815 --> 00:04:44.183
Gloria: What does it say?

00:04:44.183 --> 00:04:47.687
Caleb: The FDA studied 94
samples of arsenic in apple

00:04:47.687 --> 00:04:53.526
juice, with 95% of them having
less than 10 ppb total arsenic.

00:04:53.526 --> 00:04:57.997
All of the samples were below
10 ppb for inorganic arsenic,

00:04:57.997 --> 00:04:59.399
the more toxic kind.

00:04:59.399 --> 00:05:00.199
Gloria: So I'm good?

00:05:00.199 --> 00:05:03.236
Caleb: Yeah, the FDA is pretty
confident that apple juice is

00:05:03.236 --> 00:05:05.972
safe for kids and adults.

00:05:07.907 --> 00:05:09.776
I wonder what it
says about rice.

00:05:09.776 --> 00:05:12.278
Grace: Arsenic levels in
rice have stayed the same over

00:05:12.278 --> 00:05:14.781
the past 20 years - low.

00:05:14.781 --> 00:05:15.515
Caleb: Meaning?

00:05:15.515 --> 00:05:17.383
Grace: You don't need to
change how you eat rice.

00:05:20.520 --> 00:05:21.220
Caleb: Whew!

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:24.090
You know what that means-
I'm chowing down!

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:26.159
Gloria: It's still good
to eat different grains.

00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:27.427
Caleb: [in a matter-of-factly]
You don't know my family...

00:05:27.427 --> 00:05:28.828
Caleb: Hey mom,
what's for breakfast?

00:05:28.828 --> 00:05:29.729
Caleb's Mom: Rice.

00:05:29.729 --> 00:05:30.797
Caleb: What's for lunch?

00:05:30.797 --> 00:05:31.731
Caleb's Mom: Rice.

00:05:31.731 --> 00:05:32.665
Caleb: What's for dinner?

00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:34.033
Caleb's Mom:
Something different!

00:05:34.033 --> 00:05:36.369
Rice and beans.

00:05:36.369 --> 00:05:38.371
Gloria: [laughs]
Sounds like your mom.

00:05:38.371 --> 00:05:39.906
Gloria: I'm glad
we figured that out;

00:05:39.906 --> 00:05:41.641
I love apple juice.

00:05:41.641 --> 00:05:44.577
Thanks for looking out
for us, Grace, but relax.

00:05:44.577 --> 00:05:46.312
Grace: [Defensive] I
was just wondering!

00:05:46.312 --> 00:05:48.281
I'd seen this article
on the Internet-

00:05:48.281 --> 00:05:49.749
Caleb: Yeah, but
you can't believe

00:05:49.749 --> 00:05:51.751
everything you read online.

00:05:51.751 --> 00:05:53.486
Gloria: The sites we
looked at were good,

00:05:53.486 --> 00:05:56.889
especially the Environmental
Health Student Portal.

00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:58.658
Caleb: Can it help with
last night's homework,

00:05:58.658 --> 00:05:59.859
I still haven't done it.

00:05:59.859 --> 00:06:01.561
Grace: Do it before lunch ends!

00:06:01.561 --> 00:06:04.230
Caleb: Hold up, lemme
finish my rice real quick.

